WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:03.569
- This, I knew at
a very early age,

00:00:03.569 --> 00:00:08.208
the most cultured country in
the world built Auschwitz.

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:11.777
There's this old question,

00:00:11.777 --> 00:00:15.348 align:start size:94% position:6%
how did the people who
gave us Beethoven and Bach

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:17.249
give us Auschwitz?

00:00:17.249 --> 00:00:19.985
And I always found, it's
an interesting question,

00:00:19.985 --> 00:00:22.922
but it's somewhat
of a non-sequitur.

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:24.924 align:start size:81% position:19%
Why does having
Bach and Beethoven

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:27.994 align:start size:84% position:16%
inoculate you against
becoming evil?

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:33.333 align:start position:0%
The ease with which a
civilization can turn, morally,

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:37.437
that's what Germany taught,

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:40.673 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I see that happening
in the United States.

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:44.811
This, I always believed
was the kindest country.

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:46.746 align:start size:84% position:16%
I know it has
rotten people in it,

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:49.282
just as rotten countries
have kind people in it.

00:00:49.282 --> 00:00:54.254
But as a country, when people
wanted help after earthquakes,

00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:57.791
they didn't ask for Belgium.

00:00:57.791 --> 00:01:01.894
When people used a flag
to rebel for liberty,

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:05.431 align:start size:97% position:3%
like in Hong Kong, they
didn't take the Swedish flag.

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:07.667
But look what's happening.

00:01:08.934 --> 00:01:11.938
(mysterious music)

00:01:16.275 --> 00:01:19.912 align:start size:84% position:16%
- All right, Prager,
here we go again.

00:01:19.912 --> 00:01:22.081 align:start size:97% position:3%
I will tell the people that
we had a moment to chat

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:24.317
before we just sat down,

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:27.921
and we realized that we have
not seen each other in person

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:29.788
in about 14 or 15 months,

00:01:29.788 --> 00:01:34.126
because it was a few weeks
before my first son was born.

00:01:34.126 --> 00:01:36.095
And you said to me,

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:37.563
well, why don't I let
you tell the people

00:01:37.563 --> 00:01:39.599 align:start size:97% position:3%
what you said to me right
before the child was was born.

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:42.202
- I said I thought I had,
in fact, seen your kids.

00:01:42.202 --> 00:01:43.503
Well, there was-

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:44.804
- But you haven't as
far as I could tell.

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:46.106
So you may have dreamt
that you've seen them.

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:48.341
- Yes, so it shows you,

00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:50.143
I don't know if this
is a good or bad thing,

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:51.845
the power of photos.

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:53.246
- Yeah, I've sent you
a lot of pictures.

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:54.514
- Yes.

00:01:54.514 --> 00:01:55.482
- I sent you two
a lot of pictures.

00:01:55.482 --> 00:01:57.016
- From the beginning, exactly.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:58.251
- And then I couldn't remember
if you had seen them either.

00:01:58.251 --> 00:02:00.453 align:start size:88% position:13%
- That is really funny,
we should ask them.

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:01.587
- We should ask the kids.

00:02:01.587 --> 00:02:02.822
- [Dennis] Yes, exactly.

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:03.922
- Well, they're not
talking just yet.

00:02:03.922 --> 00:02:04.724 align:start size:84% position:16%
They're not talking.
- No, no.

00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:05.858
- Then I said to you,

00:02:05.858 --> 00:02:06.659 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Well, do you remember
holding them?"

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:07.894
Because I know, just-

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:08.995
- Yeah, that would
be a giveaway, yeah.

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:10.429
- And we have no recollection.

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:11.931 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah, I remember
holding photos.

00:02:11.931 --> 00:02:13.099
- Yeah.

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:14.300 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, okay,
that's fair enough.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.669
- But, I did see you.

00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:18.972
I truly think this
was one of the most

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:20.206
memorable nights of my life.

00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:22.475 align:start size:84% position:16%
I had you and
Jordan Peterson over

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:25.778 align:start size:97% position:3%
just two weeks or so before
my first son was born.

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:28.614 align:start size:97% position:3%
And David and I were
basically able to ask you two

00:02:28.614 --> 00:02:30.650
everything about life
and about parenting.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:31.884
- Oh, about parenting, yes.

00:02:31.884 --> 00:02:33.119
- And we talked
about everything.

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:34.953 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was one of the
most, I mean, truly,

00:02:34.953 --> 00:02:35.888 align:start size:94% position:6%
it was one of the
most meaningful
conversations in my life.

00:02:35.888 --> 00:02:37.423
- That's nice.

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:38.725
- And we realized, remember
what we realized that night,

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:39.992
about me, you, and Jordan,

00:02:39.992 --> 00:02:42.262
that we have 14 years
separating each of us.

00:02:42.262 --> 00:02:44.697 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was 46 at the
time, Jordan was 60,

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:46.366
and you were 74 at the time,

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:48.368
which is just sort
of interesting that
there's the same-

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:49.903
- Oh, that is fascinating.

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:52.939
- But you said, as, you know,
we were about to have kids,

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:55.308 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you said to me
something to the effect of,

00:02:55.308 --> 00:02:57.544
"You better enjoy it now."

00:02:57.544 --> 00:02:58.811 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah, well, I
was more specific.

00:02:58.811 --> 00:02:59.879 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, can you
clear it up for me?

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:01.114
- Well, but yes.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:06.619 align:start size:97% position:3%
Look, I'm a big, big believer
in acknowledging reality.

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:13.359 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't sugarcoat, and
I don't make worse.

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:16.596
And as I was saying
to you beforehand,

00:03:17.663 --> 00:03:21.334 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I think I might
write a column on this.

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:25.137
The notion that you can
have it all is absurd.

00:03:25.137 --> 00:03:28.707
And when the feminist
movement said to women,

00:03:28.707 --> 00:03:31.744
you could have it all, it
was one of the indications

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:33.813 align:start size:81% position:19%
that it was an
immature movement.

00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:38.350 align:start size:94% position:6%
Immature in terms of
thought and emotion, even.

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:42.621 align:start position:0%
You can have some of everything,
you can't have everything.

00:03:42.621 --> 00:03:46.559
If you want to be a
parent, and you did,

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:48.361
and Dave did, obviously,

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:51.564
that you guys, you
wanted to be parents.

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:56.469 align:start position:0%
That means that happy-go-lucky,
let's do whatever we want,

00:03:57.971 --> 00:04:00.106
anytime we want life is over.

00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:01.841 align:start size:88% position:13%
- It is over, man.
- It's over, I'm sorry.

00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:05.912 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so many immature
people, young people today,

00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:10.883
don't want to give that up.

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:14.687 align:start position:0%
They think that will bring
them happiness in the long run.

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:18.891
It's fun on any given
day, but the long run,

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:20.660
to not have children,

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:23.929 align:start size:88% position:13%
to not have the prospect
of grandchildren

00:04:23.929 --> 00:04:25.832
is such a poorer life.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.602
- Do you think you can
have it for moments,

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:31.136
have everything for moments?

00:04:31.136 --> 00:04:32.338
- Yes, that's right.

00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:33.839 align:start size:84% position:16%
- 'Cause I will
tell you one thought

00:04:33.839 --> 00:04:35.975
that I'm having a
lot lately is that,

00:04:35.975 --> 00:04:37.343
and I don't wanna jinx it,

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:38.744
I almost don't wanna
say this out loud.

00:04:38.744 --> 00:04:39.612
I'm sure there's a
Yiddish word for it.

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:41.146
- Yeah?

00:04:41.146 --> 00:04:43.716
- I have the stuff that I
want in my life right now.

00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:45.618
My life is good, my
family life is good.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:46.785
- You're blessed.

00:04:46.785 --> 00:04:48.020 align:start size:88% position:13%
- My home is good.
- That's exactly right.

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:49.122
- My work is good, my
colleagues are good.

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:50.990
- That's right, I have that.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:52.191 align:start size:84% position:16%
- But it's making
me a little nervous.

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:53.459
- Oh, oh, I don't have that.

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:54.661
- Which I've, no,
I never had that.

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:57.730 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Okay, so you are, this
is almost redundant,

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:59.932 align:start size:97% position:3%
a neurotic Jew.
- No, I'm not a neurotic Jew.

00:04:59.932 --> 00:05:00.666
- [Dennis] Okay, fine.

00:05:00.666 --> 00:05:01.901
If you think that-

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:03.302
- I'm not neurotic,
I'm not neurotic.

00:05:03.302 --> 00:05:04.536 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Okay, okay, I don't know.
- I don't have that.

00:05:04.536 --> 00:05:05.471
- I don't think you are.

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:07.473
- I don't have that thing, yeah.

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:08.741 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Oh, if you don't
have it, then,

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:09.975
oh, I thought you were
saying you do have it.

00:05:09.975 --> 00:05:11.243
- No, no, no, no, I
have this notion now-

00:05:11.243 --> 00:05:11.877
- The notion that
you're jinxing it-

00:05:11.877 --> 00:05:12.779
- Yeah.

00:05:12.779 --> 00:05:13.946
- Is neurotic.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:14.881 align:start size:81% position:19%
- That's neurotic?
- Okay, yes.

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:15.815
- Wow, we found my neurosis.

00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:17.349
- No, you're a pretty good-

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:18.150
- Because you know me, I'm
not a neurotic person as-

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:18.818
- No, that's correct.

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:19.985
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:05:19.985 --> 00:05:21.320 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You are about as
healthy a guy as I know,

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:22.321
in all seriousness.

00:05:22.321 --> 00:05:23.589
- Right.

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:27.026
- But I never, I have,
quote-unquote, it all,

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:32.899
in that sense.

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:35.168
But I don't have it all

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:37.303 align:start size:81% position:19%
because I've given
up X, Y, and Z

00:05:37.303 --> 00:05:41.574 align:start size:94% position:6%
for family, for career,
for intimacy, for friends.

00:05:41.574 --> 00:05:45.111 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, do I wanna
sleep in many Saturdays?

00:05:45.111 --> 00:05:46.378
Of course I do.

00:05:46.378 --> 00:05:48.614
But I teach at the
synagogue that I,

00:05:48.614 --> 00:05:53.452 align:start size:97% position:3%
